---
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Shell section="about" title="Accessibility" description="What ERA checks, what the audit caught, and the one exemption.">
  <h1 class="display">Accessibility</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The contrast audit lives in the Storybook rather than in a document, because it resolves what the tokens actually
    compute to for whichever brand and mode is selected. A document can claim a pass. A live audit cannot.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href={`${base}/storybook/?path=/story/foundations-accessibility--contrast-audit`}>Open the audit</a> and switch brands
    in the toolbar. The numbers move, because they are read from real computed colours rather than written down once.
  </p>

  <h2>What it caught</h2>

  <p>
    The first run failed 10 of 23 pairs. Both causes were in the tokens, not in any component, which is the argument for
    auditing the token layer rather than auditing screens.
  </p>

  <h3>The neutral ramp was carrying text</h3>

  <p>
    Placeholder sat at 1.57:1, helper text at 1.92:1, field labels at 3.32:1. All three were pointing at ramp steps that
    exist to draw borders. Measured against a surface, in both modes:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>On light</th><th>On dark</th><th>Usable for body text</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>neutral-700</code></td><td>3.32:1</td><td>3.12:1</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>neutral-800</code></td><td>3.79:1</td><td>3.76:1</td><td>No</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>neutral-900</code></td><td>5.92:1</td><td>7.67:1</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>neutral-950</code></td><td>16.29:1</td><td>13.71:1</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    So ERA has exactly two text colours per ramp, not five. That is not a gap, it is what the underlying Radix scales
    guarantee: two text steps, everything below them is for borders and fills. The consequence is worth saying plainly,
    because it changes how you build: <strong>hierarchy in text comes from size and weight, not from a third grey.</strong>
    Reaching for a lighter grey to make something feel secondary is how you end up at 3.79:1.
  </p>

  <h3>The solid brand fill had one global foreground</h3>

  <p>
    Radix splits its step 9 colours into two families: those dark enough to carry white text, and those that need dark text.
    Iris is in the first, Cyan and Jade are in the second. ERA was painting white on all three.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Fill</th><th>White on it</th><th>Near-black on it</th><th>Correct choice</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Brand 1 light</td><td><code>#5b5bd6</code></td><td>5.37:1</td><td>3.04:1</td><td>White</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Brand 1 dark</td><td><code>#5b5bd6</code></td><td>5.37:1</td><td>3.28:1</td><td>White</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Brand 2 light</td><td><code>#3db9cf</code></td><td>2.32:1</td><td>7.01:1</td><td>Near-black</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Brand 2 dark</td><td><code>#11809c</code></td><td>4.57:1</td><td>3.84:1</td><td>White</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Brand 3 light</td><td><code>#29a383</code></td><td>3.15:1</td><td>5.17:1</td><td>Near-black</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Brand 3 dark</td><td><code>#29a383</code></td><td>3.15:1</td><td>5.58:1</td><td>Near-black</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    There is no single answer in that table, which is the finding. The fix was a new brand role token,
    <code>Primary/Roles/On action</code>, so each brand answers for its own fill instead of the semantic layer guessing on
    behalf of all three.
  </p>

  <h2>The one exemption</h2>

  <p>
    The label on a disabled control is deliberately below 4.5:1. WCAG 1.4.3 exempts inactive controls, and a disabled button
    rendered at full contrast stops reading as disabled, which trades a real usability signal for a number. It moved from
    1.68:1 to 3.33:1: legible, without pretending to be active.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond contrast</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>One focus treatment.</strong> Keyboard only, drawn outside the element so it never changes layout, offset against the surface behind it so it stays visible on tinted and solid backgrounds alike.</li>
    <li><strong>Colour is never the only signal.</strong> Alert and Toast carry a distinct icon shape per tone. Badge always carries a word. Table sort state is announced through <code>aria-sort</code>, not just an arrow.</li>
    <li><strong>Native where native is better.</strong> Modal is a real <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, so focus trapping, Escape and page inertness come from the browser. Accordion is <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, so in-page find can open a closed section.</li>
    <li><strong>Correct roles where native is not used.</strong> Menu is <code>role="menu"</code> with the menu button keyboard pattern; Select is a combobox trigger over <code>role="listbox"</code> with <code>aria-activedescendant</code>. They share a surface and deliberately not their semantics.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduced motion is respected.</strong> Anything that moves does so to help someone follow a change, so when the OS asks for less motion it is removed rather than shortened.</li>
    <li><strong>The axe addon runs on every story</strong>, so labelling and role problems surface in the panel rather than in review.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is not covered</h2>

  <p>
    Honest limits. There is no screen reader testing on real assistive tech, no testing with actual users with disabilities,
    and no automated check in CI yet, so a regression would currently be caught by someone opening the audit rather than by
    the build. Automated axe runs in CI are the obvious next step.
  </p>
</Shell>
